When I work on one of my project based on STM32F411, I used this code to send data over USB:

```
// Send to USB
if(USBD_CDC_SetTxBuffer(&hUsbDeviceFS, usb_data, rx_usb_cnt) == USBD_OK)
{
    // Send packet - no waiting
    USBD_CDC_TransmitPacket(&hUsbDeviceFS);
}
```
And it works just fine regardless if USB is connected or not. When I tried use the same code on STM32F103 MCU, exactly the same code caused HardFault. Turns out that it happens because function USBD_CDC_SetTxBuffer() slightly different. This is how this function looks like for F103 MCU:
```
uint8_t  USBD_CDC_SetTxBuffer(USBD_HandleTypeDef   *pdev, uint8_t  *pbuff, uint16_t length)
{
  USBD_CDC_HandleTypeDef *hcdc = (USBD_CDC_HandleTypeDef *) pdev->pClassData;

  hcdc->TxBuffer = pbuff;
  hcdc->TxLength = length;

  return USBD_OK;
}
```
And this is how it looks like for F411 MCU:
```
uint8_t USBD_CDC_SetTxBuffer(USBD_HandleTypeDef *pdev, uint8_t *pbuff, uint32_t length)
{
  USBD_CDC_HandleTypeDef *hcdc = (USBD_CDC_HandleTypeDef *)pdev->pClassDataCmsit[pdev->classId];

  if (hcdc == NULL)
  {
    return (uint8_t)USBD_FAIL;
  }

  hcdc->TxBuffer = pbuff;
  hcdc->TxLength = length;

  return (uint8_t)USBD_OK;
}
```
As you can see, USB CDC for F411 MCU have null pointer check and return an error. As result this function can be safely called regardless in USB is connected and enumerated or not. Probably the same null pointer check should be included for F1 MCUs.

